Writer: an inline object moves with its run's w:position

Word lowers or raises an inline picture or OLE object with the
w:position of its run; MathType equations rely on it to sit on
the baseline (Gitea #9: 25 pictures and an OLE fraction stood
above their lines). The import kept w:position as a deferred
character property and applied it to text only, so an object's
run lost it.

The import now puts the position on the object's anchor
character, as a fixed escapement against that character's own
font size, and does not expand it to the text after. Layout
moves an as-character object by its anchor character's fixed
escapement (vertAlign's automatic values keep objects still,
as before). Every export already writes the anchor character's
escapement (w:position in DOCX), so the position round-trips.
The w:position arithmetic moves into one helper both use.

